  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tomorrow: Forecast remains on track for of elevated to \ncritical fire weather conditions for the Inland northwest today. \nThis mornings wave that brought virga showers and isolated lightning \nto southeastern BC has moved rapidly northeast towards central\nAlberta, bringing in a drier airmass behind it thanks to \ndownsloping winds off the Cascades and synoptic descent. Winds \nthrough much of the troposphere increase this afternoon and \nevening as a trough offshore Vancouver Island becomes neutral to\nslightly positive tilted tightening the thermal gradient over \nWA. Red flag warnings are in effect until this evening in \nportions of central WA but the area of most concern remains the \nOkanogan valley where deeper mixing and slope alignment will mix\ndown gusts to around 25-35 mph with ease. Pressure gradients \nbecome more aligned with the Cascade gaps this evening which \nwill lead to a westerly wind shift around sunset on the \nWaterville Plateau and western Columbia Basin. The Inland \nnorthwest will be brushed by a dry cold front into tomorrow \nmorning bringing an even drier airmass to central WA. However, \nthe further south and east you are tomorrow in the Inland \nNorthwest, there is a higher chance of seeing cirrus as PWATs \nare actually increasing thanks to moisture transport from the \nmonsoon high in the intermountain west. The cold front passage \nand cirrus will moderate temperatures into the 70s and 80s \ntomorrow, slightly below average. Still am expecting elevated to\nlocally critical fire weather conditions for the Okanogan \nvalley Sunday as conditions look even drier but with winds being\nweaker in the boundary layer, opted out of issuing another red \nflag warning. \n\nMonday and Tuesday: Temperatures rebound back into the 80s and 90s \nMonday and Tuesday as the upper low currently west of Vancouver \nIsland departs and subtle height rises take place. The best chance \nfor mid to high 90s will be further north and west towards central \nWA as mid and high level clouds will knock down temperatures in \neastern WA and north ID a few degrees. Locations along US-97 have a \n20-50% chance of seeing 100+ degrees Tuesday. \n\nWednesday through Sunday: Forecast confidence drops off quickly mid \nnext week as models are struggling with a shortwave trough off the \nWA/OR coast. The gridded forecast fails to tell the story. With a \nshortwave passage disrupting the monsoonal ridge, that would open \nthe door for increased chances for showers and thunderstorms, which \nwould bring cooler temperatures. For example, the LREF has a 10% \nchance of temperatures cooler than 75 degrees at Omak Thursday. On \nthe flip side, there is a 13% chance of warmer than 95 degrees. \nWednesday and Thursday will need to be closely watched for the \npotential of widespread lightning (confidence is still very low\nat this point). Interestingly enough forecast confidence \nincreases for warm and dry conditions to return as ensembles \nshow the monsoon high once again impinging on the Inland \nNorthwest beyond Thursday. /DB",
